# Quote: Mario Bunge - Treatise on Basic Philosophy - Epistemology & Methodology III - Part II

> Psychoanalysis is self-contained: it has no specific background. In particular, psychoanalysis presupposes neither biology nor experimental psychology - any contacts with which were repeatedly rejected by the master himself, e.g. in his advice to the lay analysts. True, Freud did use a number of terms borrowed from biology, starting with 'sex'; he also made frequent reference to outdated biological hypotheses, such as Lamarck's of the inheritance of acquired characters, and Haeckel's "law" that ontogeny recapitulates phylogeny. For these reasons he has been called a "cryptobiologist" (Sulloway 1979). Actually Freud's biology was phoney, because he was a dualist and because psychoanalysis ignores the nervous system.

